      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "blk_f63sjvd2bc",
          "type": "columns",
          "v": 1,
          "fields": {
            "items": [
              {
                "heading": "Governing the Commons",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "The book that won Ostrom the Nobel and dismantled Hardin’s “Tragedy of the Commons.” Eight design principles for how communities actually steward shared resources together — without private enclosure or state takeover. Foundational reading for anyone serious about commoning at any scale."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by Benjamin Life"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/governing-the-commons/A8BB63BC4A1433A50A3FB92EDBBB97D5"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "Braiding Sweetgrass",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Indigenous wisdom, scientific knowledge, and the teachings of plants — woven together. Kimmerer (citizen of the Citizen Potawatomi Nation, professor of botany) writes the bioregional ethic better than most policy papers manage. If you read one book on what "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "belonging",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " means in a place, this is it."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by Kathleen Marie Rose"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://milkweed.org/book/braiding-sweetgrass"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "Front Range Commons",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "The parallel, member-led civic body SPIRIT feeds and protects but does not control. Commoners elect a Wisdom Council, which appoints Common Stewards. Where the day-to-day work of caring for the Front Range will live. Founding membership is opening — see "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Connect.",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by SPIRIT"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://www.frontrangecommons.org/"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "The Patterning Instinct",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "A cultural history of how worldviews shape civilizations — and why the dominant Western metaphor of “nature as machine” is at the root of our ecological crisis. Useful for understanding why the Commons isn’t a policy proposal; it’s a cosmology shift."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by Benjamin Life"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://www.jeremylent.com/the-patterning-instinct.html"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "Quadratic Funding",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "The mathematical mechanism behind the Front Range Commons’ planned grant rounds: the matching pool amplifies the "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "number",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " of contributors, not the size of contributions. A wealthy donor’s $10,000 funds less than 100 neighbors each giving $5. Designed for genuine pluralism in resource allocation."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by Cameron Ely-Murdock"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://www.radicalxchange.org/concepts/quadratic-funding/"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "Sociocracy For All",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "The cleanest current resource for sociocratic decision-making. SPIRIT operates by "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "consent",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " (not majority vote) inside the Stewardship Council, with circle-level autonomy — patterns SfA documents and trains. If you want to bring sociocracy into your own organization, start here."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by Jordan Siegel"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://www.sociocracyforall.org/"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "The Listeners' Council",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "A specific gathering form: one person speaks at a time, holding a token; others listen without preparing a response. No cross-talk, no debate. The form draws from many lineages — Indigenous councils, Quaker meetings, the Way of Council. We use it at Solidarity Suppers when the conversation matters enough."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by Sarah D'Antoni"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "The Flatirons",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "The slanted sandstone slabs above Boulder — 290 million-year-old conglomerate from the Fountain Formation, lifted and tilted during the Laramide Orogeny. The signature backdrop of the Front Range and the geologic punctuation that says "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "here.",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " Walk Chautauqua at dawn."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by SPIRIT"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://bouldercolorado.gov/services/open-space-and-mountain-parks"
                }
              },
              {
                "heading": "Cache la Poudre River",
                "body": {
                  "type": "doc",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Colorado’s only nationally designated Wild & Scenic River. Headwaters in Rocky Mountain National Park; flows through Fort Collins on its way to the South Platte. The watershed that holds the northern reach of our bioregion and grounds the work of regional water stewardship coalitions."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "— Curated by SPIRIT"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "link": {
                  "type": "url",
                  "url": "https://www.fs.usda.gov/recarea/arp/recarea/?recid=36504"
                }
              }
            ],
            "columns": "2",
            "aspect": "4:5"
          }
        },
